  "response": "Attributing a bridge collapse either to a design deficiency or to substandard construction and maintenance is in the first instance a question of physical evidence, and the work therefore begins at the ruin itself, where each element is tagged by its position in the standing structure before it is lifted. Fractographic examination distinguishes fatigue cracking from single-event overload fracture, a capability produced by the recovery and cataloguing of fractured structural members from the collapse debris by forensic engineers for laboratory examination. The distinction carries weight because fatigue surfaces point to a service history of repeated loading, while a clean overload surface points to a member that was never adequate for the demand placed upon it.\n\nMaterial quality is measured rather than inferred from the appearance of the rubble. Concrete cores extracted from the wreckage are tested for compressive strength. Concrete compressive strength is determined by measuring the electrical resistivity of core samples, a method of determination that cannot be squared with the crushing of extracted cores to destruction in a press. Finite element models are built to reconstruct the load paths that existed in the structure before failure. That modelling shows that the calculated capacity of the failed connection was lower than the load demand imposed on it; in other words, the load demand on the failed connection exceeded its computed strength.\n\nThe loading condition at the instant of failure is fixed from toll records, dashboard-camera footage and the accounts of witnesses on the approaches. Exactly one of two states obtained: the bridge was carrying traffic at the moment of the collapse, or the bridge was closed to all traffic at the moment of the collapse, one of these being necessarily the case and the two being mutually exclusive. Whichever of them held governs the live load that any numerical reconstruction of the failure must reproduce.\n\nOnce the technical record is assembled, the accounts offered by the parties diverge sharply. The design engineer attributed the collapse to the contractor's departure from the specified post-tensioning sequence, whereas the contractor attributed the collapse to an under-designed truss connection node. After the design engineer had advanced that attribution, a statutory public inquiry was constituted, and such an inquiry has the power to compel witnesses to give evidence under oath. Subsequently, the commission of inquiry held that the primary cause of the collapse was a deficiency in the original design. Even though the coercive authority of such a body reaches no further than obliging witnesses to testify on oath, the findings of fact made by an official inquiry are binding on criminal courts in every jurisdiction.",
